e effects include extremist tendencies? The evidence—and the vetting built around that evidence—suggests the opposite: the ones who get out are usually the ones who rejected it hardest. I'd gently ask: if you grew up in a neighbourhood with gang violence, and it was all around you for years, would you expect people to assume that "some of it rubbed off" on you when you moved away? Or would you expect them to recognise that maybe you left precisely because you wanted no part of it?
